Z||000004||Tigger||10-03-2001||03:24 PM||||JCWhitney has suspension techniques 1 to 1-1/2" drop springs, reasonable prices, that's where I bought my rears.  They're good for bounc'in I'll tell ya!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>2" front drop spindles from Street Dreams are the way to go.||||reg||1
Z||000005||speed racer||10-03-2001||04:38 PM||||Dose any one have the suspension techniques springs if so how do they handle.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Uaana||10-03-2001||06:07 PM||||I've got the ST's with KYB struts in the rear. <BR>Haven't changed the shocks yet..<P>As for ride.. If you love being on the ground and the feel of driving a car that feels like it will never become unglued from the road... <BR>And yes it's stiffer but didn't seem all that bad to me. <P>BTW another quick improvement you can make to your handling is to upgrade your tires as well.||||reg||1
Z||000007||RedRavenGT||10-03-2001||06:53 PM||||Ive drove a GT with ST's and new Gabriel struts and shocks. Wasnt that bad at all. Ive got a set of lowering springs that Im waiting to put on till I get new struts.||||reg||1
Z||000008||samoht||10-03-2001||08:37 PM||||does anyone have the site for those lowering spindles and adjustable rear struts?||||reg||1
Z||000009||2birds||10-03-2001||10:03 PM||||I have the ST springs with full poly bushings, too.  It handles (and rides) like a go kart.  It wasn't much better, ride-quality-wise, with just the poly and stock springs.  I like it, but I'd spend the extra 40-50 bucks next time and get the Eibach's from shox.com.||||reg||1
